Cgroup allocation-domain pool reuse
==================================

Build domain-cycle with -pthread. Run in the established4K userspace ABI on a
native16K kernel. Root-visible pagemap and kpagecgroup are required for the
controller's density/charge proof. The fixture itself checks every payload byte.
No memory.high/max limits, reclaim, swap-area or GPU operations are needed.

  userpages 4k ./domain-cycle --native-16k [--tail | --race]

The program prints READY and waits for stdin byte 'c' before allocating payload.
A controller places only this fresh fixture in owned cgroup A, then acknowledges.
Each PHASE prints its index/PID/base/leaf count/stride and waits for 'c'. Both
cgroups must remain alive until all prior ranges have been checked and unmapped.

Default: three1MiB useful guarded allocation phases. Run in A, move to B, move
back to A between phases. Check all256 written leaves/range (4K every8K), excluding
the inaccessible guards. The old sticky-domain implementation can allocate16K
native backing per4K leaf after a move. Packed acceptance is1MiB..1.25MiB native
PFN union per1MiB phase; the allowance covers bounded shard/partial-owner tails.

--tail:16 independent4K allocations, switching A/B after EVERY leaf. All16VAs are
selected in the same current16-way allocator shard using its documented hash.
Eight leaves/domain should occupy at most3native folios: two useful plus one
possible preexisting partial boundary. This catches a latest-domain-only cache
that closes old A pools when switching to B, then cannot use A's surviving free
quarters on return. Preserve every earlier leaf throughout all switches. Useful
payload64KiB, maximum256KiB private backing, about8MiB virtual reservation.

--race:4workers allocate64guarded leaves each, with bounded test-only2ms pacing
per leaf. RACE prints four bases and waits for 'c'. After acknowledgement, move
only this thread group between A/B32times while allocation is active; record
monotonic move intervals. RACE_DONE prints the allocation interval and waits.
Require at least one move overlapping that interval before claiming concurrency
coverage (this does not prove a particular RCU/CAS interleaving occurred). Check
all race PFNs are charged to A or B, never outside those owned domains. Then move
the stopped fixture to B and acknowledge: it allocates one additional1MiB guarded
PHASE. Require packed density after transitions stop, plus unchanged earlier
race PFNs/charges and all bytes. Worst native payload8MiB, useful2MiB; threads
have128KiB stacks. No tight density claim applies during arbitrary in-flight moves.

For each held range, read pagemap indexed by target4K VA. A present PFN is in4K
units; native PFN=PFN>>2. Read /proc/kpagecgroup at native_PFN*8 and compare with
stat(the intended cgroup).st_ino. Old phases keep their original allocation-time
charge after process moves. During racing allocations either A/B is valid.
Re-read PFNs to reject migration during the charge sample. Do not use total
memory.current as exact payload accounting: it includes code/stacks/page tables.
Normal8KiB guard stride and --tail4KiB stride are emitted explicitly.

Use an owned fresh uncapped cgroup hierarchy and a bounded controller. If using
a parent unit and childA/B groups, move the held PID into childA BEFORE enabling
memory in that owned parent's subtree_control (parent must be empty). Do not
change unrelated cgroups or global controller settings. Parent15s alarm kills
all fixture threads; partial thread creation releases waiting workers before
joining. Controller should independently terminate/reap the owned process/unit
on protocol error, timeout or failed metadata, and retain the evidence.

--selftest only validates byte patterns and same-shard address selection; it does
not exercise cgroup moves, kernel pool allocation or RCU. Hardware qualification
must include bulk/tail/race content and exact-charge/density gates, mm-exit cleanup,
existing COW/fork/reclaim controls, and dense/concurrent timing. No source or
parser-only pass closes these runtime/performance requirements.

Race density must count newly added native owners. Before and after sampling
the final steady range, recheck the original race PFNs and allocation charges.
Reject old/new physical 4K aliases and inconsistent charges in shared native
owners. Subtract only those native owners actually observed in both stable
snapshots. The 80-owner/1,310,720-byte limit applies to the added native PFN
union for the new 1 MiB payload. Reusing an old owner's free quarters should
not count its existing live quarters as additional backing. Record total,
reused and added owners separately, plus combined per-domain quarter masks.
This is payload accounting, not proof of whole-mm alias closure.

On Neo the corrected controller observed 77 owners in the final range, of
which 14 were reused and 63 new (1,032,192 added bytes). Old mappings and
charges stayed unchanged, reused slots were disjoint and all bytes passed.
The earlier range-only oracle failed at 81 seen owners before its final
checks; that run remains failed, not retrospectively qualified.
